6140 Sep  1 01:07 build.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  14556 Sep  1 01:07 Cargo.lock\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord    850 Sep  1 01:07 Cargo.toml\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord     64 Sep  1 01:07 .claude\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord    173 Sep  1 01:07 CLAUDE.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord   1688 Sep  1 01:07 clippy.toml\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord   6295 Sep  1 01:07 CONTEXT.md\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord     84 Sep  1 01:07 docs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord 153948 Sep  1 01:07 draft.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord   2456 Sep  1 01:07 ESCALATION.md\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord    144 Sep  1 01:07 examples\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord     61 Sep  1 01:07 .git\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord     18 Sep  1 01:07 .github\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord    171 Sep  1 01:07 .gitignore\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord   1759 Sep  1 01:07 justfile\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord   1502 Sep  1 01:07 plans\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord    315 Sep  1 01:07 prelude.toy\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord   5062 Sep  1 01:07 README.md\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord   3658 Sep  1 01:07 research-log\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord     18 Sep  1 01:07 runtime\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord    326 Sep  1 01:07 site\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord    350 Sep  1 01:07 src\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord    802 Sep  1 01:07 tests\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls -la","time":{"start":1788217695865,"end":1788217695867}},"id":"prt_05a149e06001QLrq7fyrAQ5PKQ","sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","messageID":"msg_05a149633001TjN299Bq4Ldf6n"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788217695877,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","part":{"id":"prt_05a149e83001M8RQH89ez7DrLo","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"6a598f8a24b34371f9755203ce4bf243b87859cd","messageID":"msg_05a149633001TjN299Bq4Ldf6n","sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":18558,"input":11532,"output":74,"reasoning":40,"cache":{"write":0,"read":6912}},"cost":0.000880692}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788217697289,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","part":{"id":"prt_05a14a3fb0015zLAD6mlinkPbR","messageID":"msg_05a149e8c001QEwO8K728J17fM","sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","snapshot":"6a598f8a24b34371f9755203ce4bf243b87859cd","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788217697678,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8d960120a52627ab","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cat ESCALATION.md"},"output":"# Escalation: the let-bindings bug is not where the brief said it was\n\nThe brief for this lane described the issue-150 bug as the let-chain's final expression\nswallowing the next top-level statement across a line break, \"the same class of bug\" as the\n#148 fix. That is not what the scratch files show.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `scratch_let_multi.toy` and `scratch_let_single.toy` both fail with `expected end of\n  program, found {` at the `input {x: Int, y: Int}` line, after the function.\n- Removing the `input {x: Int, y: Int}` line (a program whose body is just `f(input)`, the\n  let-block unchanged) runs correctly end-to-end: the let-chain's final expression already\n  stops at the newline, exactly as the #148 same-line rule requires.\n- Replacing the let-function with a plain function and keeping the `input {x: Int, y: Int}`\n  line fails the same way. The failure has nothing to do with `let`.\n- So the failing construct is `input <type>`: a program-body line that declares what stdin\n  holds. No version of toylang parses it; `input` is an atom and the trailing record type is\n  left over.\n\n## The decision\n\nThe brief's definition of done is explicit: \"confirm BOTH scratch files run correctly\nend-to-end\". The scratch files use `input {x: Int, y: Int}` as a typed-input declaration, so\nmeeting the DoD requires making that construct parse and check. The alternative -- fixing the\nlet-chain boundary as the brief describes and leaving `input <type>` unsupported -- does not\nmeet the DoD.\n\nChosen continuation: implement `input <type>` as a minimal input-type annotation, applied the\nsame-line way a call argument is. The annotation resolves into the same `ctx.input` cell the\nuses of `input` read, so first-use-wins, validation, and all seven backends pick it up\nwithout changes of their own.\n\n## Costs\n\n- Scope: issue-150 is about `let` bindings; the annotation is a separate feature the issue\n  did not ask for. It is required only because the lane's own repro files assume it.\n- Risk: an annotation that disagrees with a position that expects a type is a loud error\n  (\"`input` is used as X here and as Y elsewhere\"), and a stream/absence/Char/Int64\n  annotation is refused the way `input_read` refuses those wire forms. No previously valid\n  program changes meaning: `input` followed by a same-line record type or type name was\n  already a parse error before this change.\n- The annotation is not extended to `inputs` or `lines`; nothing here needs it.\n","metadata":{"output":"# Escalation: the let-bindings bug is not where the brief said it was\n\nThe brief for this lane described the issue-150 bug as the let-chain's final expression\nswallowing the next top-level statement across a line break, \"the same class of bug\" as the\n#148 fix. That is not what the scratch files show.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `scratch_let_multi.toy` and `scratch_let_single.toy` both fail with `expected end of\n  program, found {` at the `input {x: Int, y: Int}` line, after the function.\n- Removing the `input {x: Int, y: Int}` line (a program whose body is just `f(input)`, the\n  let-block unchanged) runs correctly end-to-end: the let-chain's final expression already\n  stops at the newline, exactly as the #148 same-line rule requires.\n- Replacing the let-function with a plain function and keeping the `input {x: Int, y: Int}`\n  line fails the same way. The failure has nothing to do with `let`.\n- So the failing construct is `input <type>`: a program-body line that declares what stdin\n  holds. No version of toylang parses it; `input` is an atom and the trailing record type is\n  left over.\n\n## The decision\n\nThe brief's definition of done is explicit: \"confirm BOTH scratch files run correctly\nend-to-end\". The scratch files use `input {x: Int, y: Int}` as a typed-input declaration, so\nmeeting the DoD requires making that construct parse and check. The alternative -- fixing the\nlet-chain boundary as the brief describes and leaving `input <type>` unsupported -- does not\nmeet the DoD.\n\nChosen continuation: implement `input <type>` as a minimal input-type annotation, applied the\nsame-line way a call argument is. The annotation resolves into the same `ctx.input` cell the\nuses of `input` read, so first-use-wins, validation, and all seven backends pick it up\nwithout changes of their own.\n\n## Costs\n\n- Scope: issue-150 is about `let` bindings; the annotation is a separate feature the issue\n  did not ask for. It is required only because the lane's own repro files assume it.\n- Risk: an annotation that disagrees with a position that expects a type is a loud error\n  (\"`input` is used as X here and as Y elsewhere\"), and a stream/absence/Char/Int64\n  annotation is refused the way `input_read` refuses those wire forms. No previously valid\n  program changes meaning: `input` followed by a same-line record type or type name was\n  already a parse error before this change.\n- The annotation is not extended to `inputs` or `lines`; nothing here needs it.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cat ESCALATION.md","time":{"start":1788217697671,"end":1788217697676}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"There's an existing ESCALATION.md. Let me read it and the CONTEXT.md, and explore the src.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a14a4e6001NSq4i2gQ2EFyXR","sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","messageID":"msg_05a149e8c001QEwO8K728J17fM"}}
