{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788217724368,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-830cc0ba1275f5d7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git log --oneline -20"},"output":"d9896ec Add module-routing research spike (gh:162)\n0ee2e18 Regenerate corpus.json after Sink-kind merge (just check)\neb7710d Fold issue-151: dedicated Sink kind\nc73a4e4 Land issue-157: rust backend omits tl_fail helper for lines-only programs (gh:157)\n2d18e3b Emit FAIL_HELPER for lines-only programs on the rust backend (gh:157)\ne9cb3b0 Implement a dedicated Sink kind\n65c126f Board: rule on gh:156, gh:122, gh:119, gh:161 grill answers; carry gh:152/gh:102 round-2 context\n2b4fb86 dispatch-worker.sh: fail loudly instead of silently reusing an orphaned branch\n4d8d8fe Pick the stalest dead lane for the tick trigger, not the first in file order\n545dba4 Fix mail tab going blank on one malformed grill round (gh:164)\n2cfd15f Board: dispatch rust-lines-tl-fail-build (gh:157) and retire-ternary-build (gh:155)\n1787c14 prompt-experiment.md: log trial 1 (gh:151 continuation dispatch)\nba566b1 Add prompt-experiment.md: track the reassurance-phrase dispatch trial\nccfb7c3 Drop cross-tick session resume from drive-tick.sh\n489e53d Board: archive let-bindings-build, fix stale dispatch-mistake note on rust-lines-tl-fail-build\nfb7754d Board: mark rust-lines-tl-fail-build delegated, flag the dispatch mistake\n94771bb Fold dsv-parameterized-source (gh:136) into to-merge-1788204419, archive board row\nab2d53e Audit: close the tick lock fd for both pipeline stages, fix a broken policy string\nfadf269 Add router policy rule against unbounded waits\n445a25a Bound the tick pipeline so a hang cannot hold the lock forever\n","metadata":{"output":"d9896ec Add module-routing research spike (gh:162)\n0ee2e18 Regenerate corpus.json after Sink-kind merge (just check)\neb7710d Fold issue-151: dedicated Sink kind\nc73a4e4 Land issue-157: rust backend omits tl_fail helper for lines-only programs (gh:157)\n2d18e3b Emit FAIL_HELPER for lines-only programs on the rust backend (gh:157)\ne9cb3b0 Implement a dedicated Sink kind\n65c126f Board: rule on gh:156, gh:122, gh:119, gh:161 grill answers; carry gh:152/gh:102 round-2 context\n2b4fb86 dispatch-worker.sh: fail loudly instead of silently reusing an orphaned branch\n4d8d8fe Pick the stalest dead lane for the tick trigger, not the first in file order\n545dba4 Fix mail tab going blank on one malformed grill round (gh:164)\n2cfd15f Board: dispatch rust-lines-tl-fail-build (gh:157) and retire-ternary-build (gh:155)\n1787c14 prompt-experiment.md: log trial 1 (gh:151 continuation dispatch)\nba566b1 Add prompt-experiment.md: track the reassurance-phrase dispatch trial\nccfb7c3 Drop cross-tick session resume from drive-tick.sh\n489e53d Board: archive let-bindings-build, fix stale dispatch-mistake note on rust-lines-tl-fail-build\nfb7754d Board: mark rust-lines-tl-fail-build delegated, flag the dispatch mistake\n94771bb Fold dsv-parameterized-source (gh:136) into to-merge-1788204419, archive board row\nab2d53e Audit: close the tick lock fd for both pipeline stages, fix a broken policy string\nfadf269 Add router policy rule against unbounded waits\n445a25a Bound the tick pipeline so a hang cannot hold the lock forever\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git log --oneline -20","time":{"start":1788217724360,"end":1788217724365}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"This is a design document. The current compiler doesn't have GPU kernels or actual vectorization. But the issue is asking for a diagnostic-only flag that annotates which sub-expressions became \"vectorized/offloaded kernels\" and which fell back to scalar, and why (cardinality).\n\nGiven that the actual compiler doesn't have GPU kernels, I need to interpret \"vectorized/offloaded kernels\" in terms of what actually happens in the emitted code. The most concrete realization of \"vectorization/offloading\" in this compiler is likely:\n\n1. The `Map`/`Select`/fused pipeline — when a program streams (fusion), it runs as a read-one/transform-one/write-one loop. The \"offloaded kernel\" is the fused per-element pipeline.\n2. Cardinality determines admissibility: One (elementwise map), Opt (stream compaction), fold (reduction) are admissible; Stream is not.\n\nHmm, but I need to figure out what's actually implemented. Let me reconsider. This is a large design task. Let me look at how tests are structured and what \"vectorized\" might concretely map to in the emitted code.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the scope. This is a language-design project. The task says implement a `--explain-offload` build flag that is diagnostic-only. Given the actual compiler, what would this flag annotate?\n\nLet me look at the LLVM emitter which has the most concrete \"vectorizes\" concept (line 684 mentions \"is what keeps the loop in the shape that vectorises\"). And fusion (streaming pipeline). Let me understand what the \"offload boundary\" concretely is in this compiler.\n\nActually, I think I'm overcomplicating this. Let me look at what the actual implementation currently has for \"kernels\". Let me search tests and plans for a concrete notion. Let me also check the git log to understand the trajectory and recent work, and whether there's a partial implementation already.\n\nLet me look at the whole draft section 1033 onward and check plans/questions.md Q8. But first, let me understand the real code better. Let me look at the corpus to understand program shapes and the fusion concept.\n\nActually, given the complexity and the fact that this is a design-first repo, I think the concrete implementation should be: a `--explain-offload` flag that, when compiling, walks the TIR and for each relevant sub-expression reports whether it is \"vectorizable/admissible\" (based on cardinality: One/Opt/fold) or \"scalar/stream\" and why.\n\nBut this needs to connect to something real. Let me look at what \"kernel\" means in the emitted code. Let me look at the emit_llvm around line 684 and how loops are emitted, and how fusion works across backends.\n\nLet me be strategic and look at how tests are written (step tests, corpus), and check if there's an existing partial implementation or plans for this issue. Let me look at plans/ directory... wait, I'm told NOT to touch plans/. I can read though. Let me check git log and recent commits to see context.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a150cf2001InsDW046I3K1nc","sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","messageID":"msg_05a14e339001WZzR0aB67XTW3w"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788217724984,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-886feac4b6b598a1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/draft.md","limit":160,"offset":1033},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1033: \n1034: ### Backend choice\n1035: \n1036: Not a question about JSON-shaped types. Both LLVM and Cranelift bottom out in integers, floats,\n1037: vectors, pointers, and memory; neither has a string, a collector, or a number tower. Those\n1038: semantics live in the front end and the runtime, and the backend never sees a string at all,\n1039: only a pointer and a length. If semantics drift across targets it is because they were defined\n1040: in the lowering rather than above it.\n1041: \n1042: What does differ:\n1043: \n1044: ```\n1045:                      LLVM (via inkwell)              Cranelift\n1046: SIMD                 vector + scalable vector types  128-bit vector types, well tested\n1047: auto-vectorization   loop and SLP vectorizers        NONE, explicit SIMD in and out\n1048: GPU                  NVPTX, AMDGPU, SPIR-V, raw IR   none, and not on the roadmap\n1049: wasm output          wasm32 target                   none; Cranelift consumes wasm\n1050: build                pinned system LLVM, C++ chain   pure cargo\n1051: compile speed        slower                          5 to 10 times faster\n1052: ```\n1053: \n1054: Two things follow. Cranelift never vectorizes for you, which matters a great deal here, because\n1055: the whole argument above is that this design can hand a vectorizer exactly the loops it likes.\n1056: And if the browser story ever becomes WebAssembly rather than emitted JavaScript source, LLVM\n1057: runs the *same* IR through one pipeline for both native and web, which is the strongest available\n1058: guarantee that semantics do not vary by platform. Cranelift produces nothing for the web, so that\n1059: would need a second unrelated backend and the drift risk would be entirely self-policed.\n1060: \n1061: Cranelift's real advantages are build simplicity and compile speed, which matter for a REPL and\n1062: for dev builds. Using both, as rustc does, is a normal answer.\n1063: \n1064: Its GPU story is a genuine architectural mismatch rather than missing work: it assumes an SSA\n1065: control-flow graph lowered to a flat instruction stream with a conventional register allocator\n1066: and CPU calling conventions, while GPUs need divergent control flow with execution masks, a\n1067: multi-level address-space memory model, workgroup and barrier semantics, and register allocation\n1068: whose objective is occupancy. LLVM's GPU support is real but thin: it gives you the assembler and\n1069: nothing above it, so address spaces, kernel calling conventions, thread-index intrinsics, and\n1070: launch are all hand-managed.\n1071: \n1072: ### A dense tensor type\n1073: \n1074: The value model gains a seventh kind alongside null, bool, number, string, array and object: a\n1075: dense typed buffer. It is constructed explicitly, never inferred:\n1076: \n1077: ```\n1078: .readings | @f32 | reshape(1024; 3)\n1079: ```\n1080: \n1081: `@f32` narrows a JSON array of numbers into an unboxed buffer and hard-fails on heterogeneous\n1082: input, nulls, or nested strings, at the constructor rather than three stages later. It\n1083: serializes back out as nested arrays, so JSON round-tripping survives at the value level.\n1084: \n1085: `@f32` is also the second number type. This language commits to `f64` to match JSON, and `@f32`\n1086: is a deliberate lossy exit from that commitment, which is a good reason to make it a visible\n1087: operator rather than something a type inferencer decides.\n1088: \n1089: Nulls are the awkward part, since JSON has one and a dense `f32` buffer does not. **Do not use\n1090: NaN as a sentinel**, because it collides with genuine NaN. Apache Arrow already solved exactly\n1091: this with a separate validity bitmask beside the values buffer, and adopting its layout also buys\n1092: zero-copy interop with Polars, DuckDB, and pandas. For a language whose pitch is JSON processing,\n1093: that is a large return on a layout decision that has to be made anyway.\n1094: \n1095: Arithmetic on tensors broadcasts, so `$m * 2` is elementwise. That extension is confined to the\n1096: new type; plain JSON arrays keep erroring exactly as they do now.\n1097: \n1098: TODO (user): stdin and stdout should not require solving generic effect types, because they are\n1099: always string streams and so have no choice of element type. At worst the end of input shows up\n1100: at the type level, and a `ClosableStream<Str>` primitive, working the way `Opt<>` does, would be\n1101: the most trivial effect type: one that promises it is potentially not infinite in time.\n1102: \n1103: RESPONSE (agent). The pragmatic move is right and the bit being asked for already exists, but it\n1104: is named on the wrong side, and the proposal cuts against a leaning that has four arguments\n1105: behind it. Those are three separate things.\n1106: \n1107: **The bit already exists.** In the base-functor table above, `Stream<T> = nuX. 1 + T*X`, and the\n1108: `1 +` is exactly closability: it is the case where the stream stops. So `ClosableStream<T>` is\n1109: `Stream<T>` as already defined. What has no name is the *other* one, `nuX. T*X`, which never\n1110: stops. If a name is wanted for the distinction, it should attach there, because that is the case\n1111: a reduction over the stream can never produce a value for.\n1112: \n1113: **The pragmatic move is right.** stdin and stdout are byte streams with no element type to\n1114: choose, so the machinery a general `Stream<T>` needs is not needed to build them. That is a real\n1115: saving and it is the difference between starting now and waiting on\n1116: [Q1](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity).\n1117: \n1118: **The tension.** Q1 leans evaluation-level, meaning there is no `Stream<T>` *type* at all and\n1119: `Stream` is an effect annotation on an expression. A `ClosableStream<Str>` primitive is a stream\n1120: type, so taken generally it reverses that.\n1121: \n1122: The sidestep that makes it a starting point rather than a reversal: one concrete opaque built-in\n1123: is not a type constructor. A file handle is a value in languages that have no first-class\n1124: streams, and `ClosableStream<Str>` can be that, monomorphic and un-parameterisable, without\n1125: committing to `Stream<T>` being spellable over an arbitrary `T`. That is a middle position worth\n1126: naming deliberately, because arriving at it by accident would look exactly like having answered\n1127: Q1 without noticing.\n1128: \n1129: What to watch, if this is built: whatever is written against the concrete primitive has to\n1130: survive `Stream` later becoming an annotation rather than a type. The safe version keeps stdin\n1131: and stdout as opaque handles that only a small set of operations touch, so that the operations\n1132: are what generalise and the type does not have to.\n1133: \n1134: ## Strings are where platform independence actually costs something\n1135: \n1136: JavaScript strings are WTF-16: UTF-16 code units, lone surrogates permitted, with `length` and\n1137: indexing measured in code units. If the same program must mean the same thing natively and on a\n1138: JavaScript target, there are three honest options.\n1139: \n1140: **WTF-16 everywhere.** Exact JavaScript semantics, trivially identical across targets. Pays\n1141: memory and a conversion on every C FFI call natively.\n1142: \n1143: **UTF-8 everywhere, with the JavaScript-shaped API emulated.** Cheap and idiomatic natively, but\n1144: on the JavaScript target the strings cannot *be* JavaScript strings, which guts interop\n1145: ergonomics and forces conversion at every boundary.\n1146: \n1147: **Design the difference away.** Do not expose code-unit indexing or a code-unit `length` at all.\n1148: Offer iteration over scalar values and opaque indices instead. Then UTF-8 natively and UTF-16 on\n1149: the web are both conforming implementations, because no program can observe which one it got.\n1150: This is roughly Swift's move, it is the only option that is cheap on both sides, and it is a\n1151: language-design commitment that has to be made early because it constrains the string API\n1152: permanently.\n1153: \n1154: The same reasoning applies to numbers, where committing to `f64` everywhere means keeping\n1155: floating-point contraction off so the optimizer does not fuse operations behind your back, and to\n1156: object key ordering if JSON round-tripping is meant to be stable.\n1157: \n1158: ## Mutation\n1159: \n1160: Immutable values plus a small number of explicit mutable cells. Cycles can only form through a\n1161: cell, which keeps them syntactically visible.\n1162: \n1163: ```\n1164: let db2 = db.users[0].name = \"ada\"    # shadow: db unchanged, db2 is new\n1165: let c = cell(0)                        # explicit mutable cell\n1166: c <- c.get() + 1                       # in-place write\n1167: ```\n1168: \n1169: Orthogonal to cardinality.\n1170: \n1171: ### UNDECIDED: what to call the record-forming update\n1172: \n1173: In jq, `=` is not assignment. Its right-hand side is an ordinary expression, so if it yields\n1174: several values, the whole update yields several results:\n1175: \n1176: ```\n1177: {} | .a = (1,2)        # -> {a:1}, {a:2}      TWO objects, not one object with two values\n1178: ```\n1179: \n1180: That is genuinely useful. It gives config-matrix expansion, variant generation, and\n1181: property-test input enumeration for free. The problem is purely that `=` *looks* like mutation\n1182: while behaving like a record, and the multiplicity is invisible at the call site.\n1183: \n1184: Compounding it, jq's `=` and `|=` disagree about cardinality and say nothing about it:\n1185: \n1186: ```\n1187: {a:1} | .a =  (1,2)      # -> {a:1}, {a:2}    cartesian\n1188: {a:1} | .a |= (.,.+10)   # -> {a:1}           silently keeps only the first\n1189: ```\n1190: \n1191: Options under consideration:\n1192: \n\n(Showing lines 1033-1192 of 2480. Use offset=1193 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\n### Backend choice\n\nNot a question about JSON-shaped types. Both LLVM and Cranelift bottom out in integers, floats,\nvectors, pointers, and memory; neither has a string, a collector, or a number tower. Those\nsemantics live in the front end and the runtime, and the backend never sees a string at all,\nonly a pointer and a length. If semantics drift across targets it is because they were defined\nin the lowering rather than above it.\n\nWhat does differ:\n\n```\n                     LLVM (via inkwell)              Cranelift\nSIMD                 vector + scalable vector types  128-bit vector types, well tested\nauto-vectorization   loop and SLP vectorizers        NONE, explicit SIMD in and out\nGPU                  NVPTX, AMDGPU, SPIR-V, raw IR   none, and not on the roadmap\nwasm output          wasm32 target                   none; Cranelift consumes wasm\nbuild                pinned system LLVM, C++ chain   pure cargo\ncompile speed        slower                          5 to 10 times faster\n```","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/draft.md","text":"\n### Backend choice\n\nNot a question about JSON-shaped types. Both LLVM and Cranelift bottom out in integers, floats,\nvectors, pointers, and memory; neither has a string, a collector, or a number tower. Those\nsemantics live in the front end and the runtime, and the backend never sees a string at all,\nonly a pointer and a length. If semantics drift across targets it is because they were defined\nin the lowering rather than above it.\n\nWhat does differ:\n\n```\n                     LLVM (via inkwell)              Cranelift\nSIMD                 vector + scalable vector types  128-bit vector types, well tested\nauto-vectorization   loop and SLP vectorizers        NONE, explicit SIMD in and out\nGPU                  NVPTX, AMDGPU, SPIR-V, raw IR   none, and not on the roadmap\nwasm output          wasm32 target                   none; Cranelift consumes wasm\nbuild                pinned system LLVM, C++ chain   pure cargo\ncompile speed        slower                          5 to 10 times faster\n```\n\nTwo things follow. Cranelift never vectorizes for you, which matters a great deal here, because\nthe whole argument above is that this design can hand a vectorizer exactly the loops it likes.\nAnd if the browser story ever becomes WebAssembly rather than emitted JavaScript source, LLVM\nruns the *same* IR through one pipeline for both native and web, which is the strongest available\nguarantee that semantics do not vary by platform. Cranelift produces nothing for the web, so that\nwould need a second unrelated backend and the drift risk would be entirely self-policed.\n\nCranelift's real advantages are build simplicity and compile speed, which matter for a REPL and\nfor dev builds. Using both, as rustc does, is a normal answer.\n\nIts GPU story is a genuine architectural mismatch rather than missing work: it assumes an SSA\ncontrol-flow graph lowered to a flat instruction stream with a conventional register allocator\nand CPU calling conventions, while GPUs need divergent control flow with execution masks, a\nmulti-level address-space memory model, workgroup and barrier semantics, and register allocation\nwhose objective is occupancy. LLVM's GPU support is real but thin: it gives you the assembler and\nnothing above it, so address spaces, kernel calling conventions, thread-index intrinsics, and\nlaunch are all hand-managed.\n\n### A dense tensor type\n\nThe value model gains a seventh kind alongside null, bool, number, string, array and object: a\ndense typed buffer. It is constructed explicitly, never inferred:\n\n```\n.readings | @f32 | reshape(1024; 3)\n```\n\n`@f32` narrows a JSON array of numbers into an unboxed buffer and hard-fails on heterogeneous\ninput, nulls, or nested strings, at the constructor rather than three stages later. It\nserializes back out as nested arrays, so JSON round-tripping survives at the value level.\n\n`@f32` is also the second number type. This language commits to `f64` to match JSON, and `@f32`\nis a deliberate lossy exit from that commitment, which is a good reason to make it a visible\noperator rather than something a type inferencer decides.\n\nNulls are the awkward part, since JSON has one and a dense `f32` buffer does not. **Do not use\nNaN as a sentinel**, because it collides with genuine NaN. Apache Arrow already solved exactly\nthis with a separate validity bitmask beside the values buffer, and adopting its layout also buys\nzero-copy interop with Polars, DuckDB, and pandas. For a language whose pitch is JSON processing,\nthat is a large return on a layout decision that has to be made anyway.\n\nArithmetic on tensors broadcasts, so `$m * 2` is elementwise. That extension is confined to the\nnew type; plain JSON arrays keep erroring exactly as they do now.\n\nTODO (user): stdin and stdout should not require solving generic effect types, because they are\nalways string streams and so have no choice of element type. At worst the end of input shows up\nat the type level, and a `ClosableStream<Str>` primitive, working the way `Opt<>` does, would be\nthe most trivial effect type: one that promises it is potentially not infinite in time.\n\nRESPONSE (agent). The pragmatic move is right and the bit being asked for already exists, but it\nis named on the wrong side, and the proposal cuts against a leaning that has four arguments\nbehind it. Those are three separate things.\n\n**The bit already exists.** In the base-functor table above, `Stream<T> = nuX. 1 + T*X`, and the\n`1 +` is exactly closability: it is the case where the stream stops. So `ClosableStream<T>` is\n`Stream<T>` as already defined. What has no name is the *other* one, `nuX. T*X`, which never\nstops. If a name is wanted for the distinction, it should attach there, because that is the case\na reduction over the stream can never produce a value for.\n\n**The pragmatic move is right.** stdin and stdout are byte streams with no element type to\nchoose, so the machinery a general `Stream<T>` needs is not needed to build them. That is a real\nsaving and it is the difference between starting now and waiting on\n[Q1](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity).\n\n**The tension.** Q1 leans evaluation-level, meaning there is no `Stream<T>` *type* at all and\n`Stream` is an effect annotation on an expression. A `ClosableStream<Str>` primitive is a stream\ntype, so taken generally it reverses that.\n\nThe sidestep that makes it a starting point rather than a reversal: one concrete opaque built-in\nis not a type constructor. A file handle is a value in languages that have no first-class\nstreams, and `ClosableStream<Str>` can be that, monomorphic and un-parameterisable, without\ncommitting to `Stream<T>` being spellable over an arbitrary `T`. That is a middle position worth\nnaming deliberately, because arriving at it by accident would look exactly like having answered\nQ1 without noticing.\n\nWhat to watch, if this is built: whatever is written against the concrete primitive has to\nsurvive `Stream` later becoming an annotation rather than a type. The safe version keeps stdin\nand stdout as opaque handles that only a small set of operations touch, so that the operations\nare what generalise and the type does not have to.\n\n## Strings are where platform independence actually costs something\n\nJavaScript strings are WTF-16: UTF-16 code units, lone surrogates permitted, with `length` and\nindexing measured in code units. If the same program must mean the same thing natively and on a\nJavaScript target, there are three honest options.\n\n**WTF-16 everywhere.** Exact JavaScript semantics, trivially identical across targets. Pays\nmemory and a conversion on every C FFI call natively.\n\n**UTF-8 everywhere, with the JavaScript-shaped API emulated.** Cheap and idiomatic natively, but\non the JavaScript target the strings cannot *be* JavaScript strings, which guts interop\nergonomics and forces conversion at every boundary.\n\n**Design the difference away.** Do not expose code-unit indexing or a code-unit `length` at all.\nOffer iteration over scalar values and opaque indices instead. Then UTF-8 natively and UTF-16 on\nthe web are both conforming implementations, because no program can observe which one it got.\nThis is roughly Swift's move, it is the only option that is cheap on both sides, and it is a\nlanguage-design commitment that has to be made early because it constrains the string API\npermanently.\n\nThe same reasoning applies to numbers, where committing to `f64` everywhere means keeping\nfloating-point contraction off so the optimizer does not fuse operations behind your back, and to\nobject key ordering if JSON round-tripping is meant to be stable.\n\n## Mutation\n\nImmutable values plus a small number of explicit mutable cells. Cycles can only form through a\ncell, which keeps them syntactically visible.\n\n```\nlet db2 = db.users[0].name = \"ada\"    # shadow: db unchanged, db2 is new\nlet c = cell(0)                        # explicit mutable cell\nc <- c.get() + 1                       # in-place write\n```\n\nOrthogonal to cardinality.\n\n### UNDECIDED: what to call the record-forming update\n\nIn jq, `=` is not assignment. Its right-hand side is an ordinary expression, so if it yields\nseveral values, the whole update yields several results:\n\n```\n{} | .a = (1,2)        # -> {a:1}, {a:2}      TWO objects, not one object with two values\n```\n\nThat is genuinely useful. It gives config-matrix expansion, variant generation, and\nproperty-test input enumeration for free. The problem is purely that `=` *looks* like mutation\nwhile behaving like a record, and the multiplicity is invisible at the call site.\n\nCompounding it, jq's `=` and `|=` disagree about cardinality and say nothing about it:\n\n```\n{a:1} | .a =  (1,2)      # -> {a:1}, {a:2}    cartesian\n{a:1} | .a |= (.,.+10)   # -> {a:1}           silently keeps only the first\n```\n\nOptions under consideration:\n","lineStart":1033,"lineEnd":1192,"totalLines":2480,"truncated":true}},"title":"draft.md","time":{"start":1788217724965,"end":1788217724981}},"id":"prt_05a150dbc001ezs39bZE4KAU85","sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","messageID":"msg_05a14e339001WZzR0aB67XTW3w"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788217724994,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","part":{"id":"prt_05a15103f001l94JZ3wIcCfVDp","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"6a598f8a24b34371f9755203ce4bf243b87859cd","messageID":"msg_05a14e339001WZzR0aB67XTW3w","sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":48384,"input":4078,"output":47,"reasoning":739,"cache":{"write":0,"read":43520}},"cost":0.00110287}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788217725975,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","part":{"id":"prt_05a15140d00187HHakiZ5cTsmu","messageID":"msg_05a151049001tiC2zxwmXue42C","sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","snapshot":"6a598f8a24b34371f9755203ce4bf243b87859cd","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788217739259,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8cd129dcad6e8d3a","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cat tests/step_1.rs tests/corpus.rs"},"output":"fn err(src: &str) -> String {\n    toylang::compile(src).map(|_| ()).unwrap_err().to_string()\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn hello_world() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(toylang::run(r#\"\"hello world\"\"#).unwrap());\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn emitted_lua() {\n    let p = toylang::compile(r#\"\"hello world\"\"#).unwrap();\n    let (lua, ty) = (toylang::emit_lua::emit(&p), p.body.ty.clone());\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(format!(\"-- : {ty}\\n{lua}\"));\n}\n\n/// Escapes survive the trip through the Lua emitter rather than terminating the literal early.\n#[test]\nfn quotes_and_newlines_round_trip() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(toylang::run(r#\"\"say \\\"hi\\\"\\n\\tand \\\\ too\"\"#).unwrap());\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn unterminated_string() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(err(r#\"\"oops\"#));\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn trailing_garbage() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(err(r#\"\"a\" \"b\"\"#));\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn empty_program() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"   \"));\n}\n\n/// Both edges at the dimension's own boundaries is the identity `[]` already is, so the\n/// both-omitted slice is refused rather than carried as a spelling for it.\n#[test]\nfn slice_with_no_bounds_is_refused() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"[1, 2, 3][:]\"));\n}\n//! The agreement harness.\n//!\n//! One corpus, every backend. This is the check a single-backend suite cannot express: two\n//! backends producing different answers is a failure in its own right, separate from either of\n//! them producing a wrong one. The step_*.rs suites keep their own job, which is pinning error\n//! messages and emitted code; this is about behaviour, and behaviour is what has to be identical\n//! across targets.\n\nmod support;\n\n/// The agreement check for one slice of the corpus: every `of`th case by index. Splitting by\n/// index, not by backend, is the point -- `agreement_failures` runs every backend internally, so\n/// the case is the unit of work a parallel runner can hand to a thread, and each shard keeps the\n/// same bar a single test always had.\nfn assert_shard_agrees(shard: usize, of: usize) {\n    let cases = support::cases();\n    assert!(\n        !cases.is_empty(),\n        \"the corpus is empty, so this test proves nothing\"\n    );\n\n    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n\n    for (i, case) in cases.iter().enumerate() {\n        if i % of != shard {\n            continue;\n        }\n        failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n            &case.name,\n            &case.program,\n            case.input.as_deref(),\n            &case.expect,\n        ));\n    }\n\n    assert!(\n        failures.is_empty(),\n        \"{} corpus programs failed in shard {shard}/{of}:\\n{}\",\n        failures.len(),\n        failures.join(\"\\n\")\n    );\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_0() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(0, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_1() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(1, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_2() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(2, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_3() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(3, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_4() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(4, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_5() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(5, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_6() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(6, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_7() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(7, 8);\n}\n\n/// What a program prints is not always the whole claim. A case that asks for it gets the code a\n/// backend emitted pinned as well, which is how a property invisible in the output -- a name\n/// that has to be declared, a type that has to be spelled -- stays observed.\n#[test]\nfn emitted_code_matches_the_snapshot() {\n    let mut asked = 0;\n    for case in support::cases() {\n        let program =\n            toylang::compile(&case.program).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", case.name));\n        for backend in case.snapshot {\n            let emitted = backend.emit(&program).unwrap_or_else(|e| {\n                panic!(\"{}: {} could not emit: {e}\", case.name, backend.name())\n            });\n            insta::assert_snapshot!(format!(\"{}__{}\", case.name, backend.name()), emitted);\n            asked += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    assert!(\n        asked > 0,\n        \"no case asks for a snapshot, so this test proves nothing\"\n    );\n}\n","metadata":{"output":"fn err(src: &str) -> String {\n    toylang::compile(src).map(|_| ()).unwrap_err().to_string()\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn hello_world() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(toylang::run(r#\"\"hello world\"\"#).unwrap());\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn emitted_lua() {\n    let p = toylang::compile(r#\"\"hello world\"\"#).unwrap();\n    let (lua, ty) = (toylang::emit_lua::emit(&p), p.body.ty.clone());\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(format!(\"-- : {ty}\\n{lua}\"));\n}\n\n/// Escapes survive the trip through the Lua emitter rather than terminating the literal early.\n#[test]\nfn quotes_and_newlines_round_trip() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(toylang::run(r#\"\"say \\\"hi\\\"\\n\\tand \\\\ too\"\"#).unwrap());\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn unterminated_string() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(err(r#\"\"oops\"#));\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn trailing_garbage() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(err(r#\"\"a\" \"b\"\"#));\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn empty_program() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"   \"));\n}\n\n/// Both edges at the dimension's own boundaries is the identity `[]` already is, so the\n/// both-omitted slice is refused rather than carried as a spelling for it.\n#[test]\nfn slice_with_no_bounds_is_refused() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"[1, 2, 3][:]\"));\n}\n//! The agreement harness.\n//!\n//! One corpus, every backend. This is the check a single-backend suite cannot express: two\n//! backends producing different answers is a failure in its own right, separate from either of\n//! them producing a wrong one. The step_*.rs suites keep their own job, which is pinning error\n//! messages and emitted code; this is about behaviour, and behaviour is what has to be identical\n//! across targets.\n\nmod support;\n\n/// The agreement check for one slice of the corpus: every `of`th case by index. Splitting by\n/// index, not by backend, is the point -- `agreement_failures` runs every backend internally, so\n/// the case is the unit of work a parallel runner can hand to a thread, and each shard keeps the\n/// same bar a single test always had.\nfn assert_shard_agrees(shard: usize, of: usize) {\n    let cases = support::cases();\n    assert!(\n        !cases.is_empty(),\n        \"the corpus is empty, so this test proves nothing\"\n    );\n\n    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n\n    for (i, case) in cases.iter().enumerate() {\n        if i % of != shard {\n            continue;\n        }\n        failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n            &case.name,\n            &case.program,\n            case.input.as_deref(),\n            &case.expect,\n        ));\n    }\n\n    assert!(\n        failures.is_empty(),\n        \"{} corpus programs failed in shard {shard}/{of}:\\n{}\",\n        failures.len(),\n        failures.join(\"\\n\")\n    );\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_0() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(0, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_1() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(1, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_2() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(2, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_3() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(3, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_4() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(4, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_5() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(5, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_6() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(6, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_7() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(7, 8);\n}\n\n/// What a program prints is not always the whole claim. A case that asks for it gets the code a\n/// backend emitted pinned as well, which is how a property invisible in the output -- a name\n/// that has to be declared, a type that has to be spelled -- stays observed.\n#[test]\nfn emitted_code_matches_the_snapshot() {\n    let mut asked = 0;\n    for case in support::cases() {\n        let program =\n            toylang::compile(&case.program).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", case.name));\n        for backend in case.snapshot {\n            let emitted = backend.emit(&program).unwrap_or_else(|e| {\n                panic!(\"{}: {} could not emit: {e}\", case.name, backend.name())\n            });\n            insta::assert_snapshot!(format!(\"{}__{}\", case.name, backend.name()), emitted);\n            asked += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    assert!(\n        asked > 0,\n        \"no case asks for a snapshot, so this test proves nothing\"\n    );\n}\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cat tests/step_1.rs tests/corpus.rs","time":{"start":1788217739252,"end":1788217739256}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"This is a design document. The actual implementation needs to map these concepts to concrete code. I need to figure out what \"vectorized/offloaded kernel\" concretely maps to in the current compiler.\n\nLet me reconsider. The current compiler emits code for 7 backends. There's a `fusion` concept (streaming pipeline). The LLVM backend has vectorization considerations. But there is no GPU kernel dispatch in the current compiler.\n\nGiven this is a diagnostic-only flag (no type-system or signature change), I think the realistic interpretation is:\n\nThe flag annotates, per sub-expression in the compiled program, whether it's \"admissible\" (vectorizable/offloadable) or falls back to scalar, based on cardinality. In the current compiler, the concrete notion of \"offloading\" is most strongly realized in the **fusion/streaming** machinery — a stream-typed pipeline that fuses into a read-one/transform-one/write-one loop. And the \"kernel-admissibility predicate\" is about cardinality.\n\nHmm, but actually the more I think, I suspect the concrete realization should be simpler and more tractable. Let me look at what data the compiler already has about cardinality of sub-expressions.\n\nLet me reconsider: perhaps the cleanest interpretation given the current compiler is:\n\nThe `--explain-offload` flag walks the TIR. For each node that represents a \"kernel candidate\" — the Map/Select stages of a fused pipeline, and reductions (fold/builtins like sum/max), etc. — it reports whether that node is vectorizable (admissible) or falls back to scalar, and why.\n\nBut without GPU kernels, \"vectorized kernel\" must mean something concrete. Let me look at how the LLVM backend emits loops and whether there's a notion of \"kernel\" vs \"scalar\". Let me look at emit_llvm.rs around the vectorize comment.\n\nActually, let me step back and reconsider the whole thing. This is a \"design project\" with a massive draft.md. The issue #168 is about a diagnostic flag. Given that the current compiler doesn't actually offload to GPU, the \"explain-offload\" flag is a *design artifact* — but the DoD says \"implementation complete; just check green.\" So there must be a concrete implementation.\n\nLet me look more carefully at what actually happens. Let me examine the corpus tests to understand program shapes, and look at how tests are structured (step tests with insta snapshots). Let me also re-read the issue: \"compiled output is annotated (e.g. printed alongside normal output, or to stderr) with which sub-expressions became vectorized/offloaded kernels and which fell back to scalar evaluation, and why (cardinality: One/Opt/fold are admissible, Stream is not).\"\n\nSo I need to:\n1. Add a `--explain-offload` flag to the CLI (main.rs).\n2. When passed, print annotations about which sub-expressions became vectorized/offloaded kernels vs scalar fallback, and why based on cardinality.\n\nThe concrete \"vectorized/offloaded kernels\" in this compiler = the fused per-element pipeline stages (Map/Select over a stream/Vec). These run in a tight loop (a \"kernel\"). A sub-expression that is part of an elementwise map over a collection is \"vectorizable\". A sub-expression that is scalar (evaluated once) is not offloaded. A sub-expression over a Stream (unbounded) is not admissible.\n\nHmm. Let me look at what types/cardinality info the TIR carries. Each node has `ty: Type`. So I can classify each node's cardinality by its type:\n- Type::Stream(_) -> Stream -> NOT admissible (must materialize / scalar fallback)\n- Type::Vec(_) -> known cardinality (fold/One) -> admissible\n- Opt -> admissible (stream compaction)\n- scalars (Int, Str, etc.) -> One -> admissible\n\nWait, but the issue says \"which sub-expressions became vectorized/offloaded kernels and which fell back to scalar evaluation.\" This suggests there's a notion of a sub-expression being *chosen* as a kernel vs falling back. In the current compiler, what's the analog?\n\nLet me look at how the LLVM backend handles \"vectorizes\". Actually I bet the real, concrete realization is simpler. Let me look at what tests exist that I could model a test on. Let me look at the corpus and a couple of test files, and understand the test harness.\n\nLet me look at tests/corpus.rs and tests/streams.rs and one step test.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a1546ae001VnKlAAz5HMEGu7","sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","messageID":"msg_05a151049001tiC2zxwmXue42C"}}
