{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788217744370,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a056dedff36dd623","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls tests/support && cat tests/support/mod.rs 2>/dev/null | head -80"},"output":"mod.rs\n//! Loading the corpus.\n//!\n//! One case per file. A program, the input it reads, the output it must produce and whatever\n//! else it asks for are one thing, and were three files that only a shared stem tied together:\n//! a program whose `.out` was missing was a panic at load, and a `.in.json` whose stem was\n//! misspelt was silently a program that reads no input.\n//!\n//! Read by both the agreement harness and the native backend's tracker, which were two copies\n//! of the same directory walk.\n\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nuse std::path::{Path, PathBuf};\n\nuse serde::Deserialize;\n\n/// The file as written. `deny_unknown_fields` is the point of the struct: a misspelt key would\n/// otherwise be a case that quietly asks for nothing extra, which looks exactly like a case that\n/// passes.\n#[derive(Deserialize)]\n#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]\nstruct Fields {\n    program: String,\n    #[serde(default)]\n    input: Option<String>,\n    #[serde(default)]\n    output: Option<String>,\n    /// Set when the program compiles but every backend has to refuse to run it. What each says\n    /// while refusing is its own business, so only the refusal is compared.\n    #[serde(default)]\n    refuses: bool,\n    /// Backends whose emitted code is snapshotted for this program, for cases where what the\n    /// program prints is not the whole claim. Empty for most cases: running on every backend and\n    /// agreeing is the ordinary bar, and a snapshot on top of it is the exception.\n    #[serde(default)]\n    snapshot: Vec<String>,\n    /// The AST shapes this program exercises, in `toylang::tags` terms. Generated by\n    /// `tests/tag_corpus.rs` on every run, not hand-authored, so it is read here only to keep\n    /// `deny_unknown_fields` from rejecting it -- the fresh value always comes from `toylang::tags`.\n    #[serde(default)]\n    node_types: Vec<String>,\n}\n\n/// What every backend has to do with the program. A case says exactly one of these, because a\n/// case that says both, or neither, is one nobody wrote down the point of.\npub enum Expect {\n    /// Every backend prints this, and they have to agree.\n    Output(String),\n    /// Every backend refuses to run it.\n    Refusal,\n}\n\npub struct Case {\n    pub name: String,\n    pub program: String,\n    pub input: Option<String>,\n    pub expect: Expect,\n    pub snapshot: Vec<toylang::Backend>,\n}\n\n/// Runs `program` on every backend and reports how the run fell short of `expect`, one line per\n/// failure, empty when every backend did what the case asked. The corpus harness and the docs\n/// harness both feed programs through this: a docs fragment is a corpus case defined in prose,\n/// so what \"agrees\" means must be one definition, not two.\npub fn agreement_failures(\n    name: &str,\n    program: &str,\n    input: Option<&str>,\n    expect: &Expect,\n) -> Vec<String> {\n    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n\n    if let Expect::Refusal = expect {\n        for backend in toylang::Backend::ALL {\n            if let Ok(out) = toylang::run_on(program, input, backend) {\n                failures.push(format!(\n                    \"RAN     {name}: {} produced {out:?} instead of refusing\",\n                    backend.name()\n                ));\n            }\n","metadata":{"output":"mod.rs\n//! Loading the corpus.\n//!\n//! One case per file. A program, the input it reads, the output it must produce and whatever\n//! else it asks for are one thing, and were three files that only a shared stem tied together:\n//! a program whose `.out` was missing was a panic at load, and a `.in.json` whose stem was\n//! misspelt was silently a program that reads no input.\n//!\n//! Read by both the agreement harness and the native backend's tracker, which were two copies\n//! of the same directory walk.\n\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nuse std::path::{Path, PathBuf};\n\nuse serde::Deserialize;\n\n/// The file as written. `deny_unknown_fields` is the point of the struct: a misspelt key would\n/// otherwise be a case that quietly asks for nothing extra, which looks exactly like a case that\n/// passes.\n#[derive(Deserialize)]\n#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]\nstruct Fields {\n    program: String,\n    #[serde(default)]\n    input: Option<String>,\n    #[serde(default)]\n    output: Option<String>,\n    /// Set when the program compiles but every backend has to refuse to run it. What each says\n    /// while refusing is its own business, so only the refusal is compared.\n    #[serde(default)]\n    refuses: bool,\n    /// Backends whose emitted code is snapshotted for this program, for cases where what the\n    /// program prints is not the whole claim. Empty for most cases: running on every backend and\n    /// agreeing is the ordinary bar, and a snapshot on top of it is the exception.\n    #[serde(default)]\n    snapshot: Vec<String>,\n    /// The AST shapes this program exercises, in `toylang::tags` terms. Generated by\n    /// `tests/tag_corpus.rs` on every run, not hand-authored, so it is read here only to keep\n    /// `deny_unknown_fields` from rejecting it -- the fresh value always comes from `toylang::tags`.\n    #[serde(default)]\n    node_types: Vec<String>,\n}\n\n/// What every backend has to do with the program. A case says exactly one of these, because a\n/// case that says both, or neither, is one nobody wrote down the point of.\npub enum Expect {\n    /// Every backend prints this, and they have to agree.\n    Output(String),\n    /// Every backend refuses to run it.\n    Refusal,\n}\n\npub struct Case {\n    pub name: String,\n    pub program: String,\n    pub input: Option<String>,\n    pub expect: Expect,\n    pub snapshot: Vec<toylang::Backend>,\n}\n\n/// Runs `program` on every backend and reports how the run fell short of `expect`, one line per\n/// failure, empty when every backend did what the case asked. The corpus harness and the docs\n/// harness both feed programs through this: a docs fragment is a corpus case defined in prose,\n/// so what \"agrees\" means must be one definition, not two.\npub fn agreement_failures(\n    name: &str,\n    program: &str,\n    input: Option<&str>,\n    expect: &Expect,\n) -> Vec<String> {\n    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n\n    if let Expect::Refusal = expect {\n        for backend in toylang::Backend::ALL {\n            if let Ok(out) = toylang::run_on(program, input, backend) {\n                failures.push(format!(\n                    \"RAN     {name}: {} produced {out:?} instead of refusing\",\n                    backend.name()\n                ));\n            }\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls tests/support && cat tests/support/mod.rs 2>/dev/null | head -80","time":{"start":1788217744363,"end":1788217744366}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the fusion/streaming concept well. Let me look at a corpus file to understand the YAML format and how tests exercise the flag. And let me look at support/helpers.\n\nLet me look at tests/support and one corpus yaml (map_after_select, sum_ints).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a155a44001pEV0sZridwNt5P","sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","messageID":"msg_05a15533d0010LfvT1ToBA8451"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788217744770,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-bf99c57aa10b9457","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cat tests/corpus/map_after_select.yaml tests/corpus/sum_ints.yaml tests/corpus/jsonlines_of_inputs.yaml"},"output":"program: |\n  [1, 2, 3] | select(. >= 2) | map(. * 10)\n\noutput: |\n  [20,30]\n\nnode_types: [arith.mul, compare.ge, int, local, map-over, pipe, selection.narrow, vec-literal]\nprogram: |\n  sum([1, 2, 3, 4])\n\noutput: |\n  10\n\nnode_types: [builtin.sum, int, vec-literal]\n# The one shape the compiler fuses into a read-one/transform-one/write-one loop instead of\n# reading all of stdin before printing anything: `jsonlines f inputs` where `f`'s own body is\n# a `map`/`select` chain over its parameter. Output is unaffected either way -- this case only\n# pins the value; tests/streaming.rs is what checks output actually arrives before stdin closes.\nprogram: |\n  fn adults(users: Stream<{name: Str, age: Int}>) -> Stream<{name: Str}> =\n      users | select(.age >= 18) | map {name: .name}\n\n  jsonlines adults inputs\n\ninput: |\n  {\"name\": \"ada\", \"age\": 36}\n  {\"name\": \"bo\", \"age\": 9}\n  {\"name\": \"cy\", \"age\": 21}\n\noutput: |\n  {\"name\":\"ada\"}\n  {\"name\":\"cy\"}\n\nnode_types: [application, builtin.jsonlines, compare.ge, inputs, int, local, map-over, pipe, projection, record-literal, selection.narrow, var]\n","metadata":{"output":"program: |\n  [1, 2, 3] | select(. >= 2) | map(. * 10)\n\noutput: |\n  [20,30]\n\nnode_types: [arith.mul, compare.ge, int, local, map-over, pipe, selection.narrow, vec-literal]\nprogram: |\n  sum([1, 2, 3, 4])\n\noutput: |\n  10\n\nnode_types: [builtin.sum, int, vec-literal]\n# The one shape the compiler fuses into a read-one/transform-one/write-one loop instead of\n# reading all of stdin before printing anything: `jsonlines f inputs` where `f`'s own body is\n# a `map`/`select` chain over its parameter. Output is unaffected either way -- this case only\n# pins the value; tests/streaming.rs is what checks output actually arrives before stdin closes.\nprogram: |\n  fn adults(users: Stream<{name: Str, age: Int}>) -> Stream<{name: Str}> =\n      users | select(.age >= 18) | map {name: .name}\n\n  jsonlines adults inputs\n\ninput: |\n  {\"name\": \"ada\", \"age\": 36}\n  {\"name\": \"bo\", \"age\": 9}\n  {\"name\": \"cy\", \"age\": 21}\n\noutput: |\n  {\"name\":\"ada\"}\n  {\"name\":\"cy\"}\n\nnode_types: [application, builtin.jsonlines, compare.ge, inputs, int, local, map-over, pipe, projection, record-literal, selection.narrow, var]\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cat tests/corpus/map_after_select.yaml tests/corpus/sum_ints.yaml tests/corpus/jsonlines_of_inputs.yaml","time":{"start":1788217744767,"end":1788217744769}},"id":"prt_05a155be5001Z842MxWJOaagLj","sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","messageID":"msg_05a15533d0010LfvT1ToBA8451"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788217744784,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","part":{"id":"prt_05a155d8e001lJ8urLqneqSPXz","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"6a598f8a24b34371f9755203ce4bf243b87859cd","messageID":"msg_05a15533d0010LfvT1ToBA8451","sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":57776,"input":3577,"output":117,"reasoning":66,"cache":{"write":0,"read":54016}},"cost":0.001129701}}
