{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788217856550,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b3f78ce76c34ceeb","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"include":"*.rs","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/src","pattern":"pub fn (contains_vec|contains_stream|as_opt|elem)"},"output":"Found 4 matches\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/src/ty.rs:\n  Line 118:     pub fn contains_stream(&self) -> bool {\n\n  Line 148:     pub fn elem(&self) -> Option<&Type> {\n\n  Line 167:     pub fn as_opt(&self) -> Option<&Type> {\n\n  Line 237:     pub fn contains_vec(&self) -> bool {\n","metadata":{"matches":4,"truncated":false},"title":"pub fn (contains_vec|contains_stream|as_opt|elem)","time":{"start":1788217856518,"end":1788217856546}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"This is a very large and intricate codebase. I now have enough understanding. Let me settle on the implementation approach and be pragmatic.\n\nGiven the design intent and the actual compiler, I'll implement `--explain-offload` as a diagnostic that walks the TIR and, for each sub-expression, reports its cardinality and whether that makes it admissible for offload (vectorized kernel) or not (scalar fallback), with the reason. This directly implements the issue: \"which sub-expressions became vectorized/offloaded kernels and which fell back to scalar evaluation, and why (cardinality: One/Opt/fold are admissible, Stream is not).\"\n\nLet me define cardinality of a type:\n- `Type::Stream(_)` -> Stream (not admissible)\n- contains Vec (known cardinality collection) -> Fold\n- Opt -> Opt\n- otherwise (scalar) -> One\n\nActually, to keep it clean and directly per the issue's cardinality list (One/Opt/fold/Stream), let me define a `cardinality(ty) -> Cardinality`:\n- Stream(_) -> Stream\n- Vec(_) -> Fold (known cardinality)  -- the issue says \"fold\" for the collection\n- Opt (as_opt) -> Opt\n- scalar -> One\n\nThen admissibility = cardinality != Stream. Reason = \"Stream: unbounded cardinality\".\n\nNow the report: walk the whole TIR tree, and for each node emit a line describing the node, its cardinality, and whether it's \"vectorized/offloaded\" (admissible) or \"scalar fallback\" (Stream). \n\nBut this would report every scalar literal as \"vectorized/offloaded\", which is semantically odd (\"a literal became a vectorized kernel\"). The issue's phrasing suggests distinguishing kernel-worthy expressions from scalar ones. Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider once more, focusing on what's most defensible and useful. I think the cleanest, most honest framing that matches BOTH the issue and the actual compiler is to report, per sub-expression, its cardinality and its admissibility, where \"admissible\" means \"would be offloaded/vectorized\" and \"not admissible\" (Stream) means \"falls back to scalar evaluation\". The cardinality is the \"why\". Scalars (One) and Opts and collections (fold) are all admissible; Stream is not.\n\nBut to avoid the absurdity of calling a literal a \"kernel\", I'll phrase lines as:\n`<node> : <cardinality> -- <admissible ? \"offloadable (vectorized kernel)\" : \"falls back to scalar\">`\n\nFor scalars, cardinality One is admissible. That's fine — the design says \"admissible = scalars, and anything of known cardinality\". So a scalar IS admissible. Good, the design explicitly says scalars are admissible. So calling a scalar \"offloadable\" is consistent with the design (\"scalars... admissible\").\n\nWait, but the issue says \"which sub-expressions became vectorized/offloaded kernels and which fell back to scalar evaluation\". \"Fell back to scalar\" = the Stream ones (they can't be vectorized so they fall back to scalar eval). The admissible ones (including scalars) are \"vectorized/offloaded\". Per the design, a scalar is admissible (part of the value layer / offload candidate). OK this is consistent enough.\n\nNow, should I report every node? For a diagnostic, reporting every node of a small test program is fine. But let me reduce noise by only reporting nodes at the \"expression boundaries\" — actually let me just report all nodes; it's a diagnostic and completeness is the point (\"which sub-expressions became... which fell back\"). I'll walk the tree and print one line per node with indentation for readability.\n\nActually, a cleaner approach: report one line per node with a leading marker. Since TIR has no spans, I'll identify nodes by kind and a short type. Let me produce lines like:\n\n```\noffload: <kind> : <ty> -> <cardinality> -> offloadable | scalar fallback\n```\n\nLet me now write the module. I'll make a `Cardinality` enum with `One`, `Opt`, `Fold`, `Stream`, and a `label()` and `admissible()`.\n\nLet me design the `explain(program) -> String` output format. I'll walk each function body and the main body, printing indented pre-order lines. For each node:\n- compute cardinality from node.ty\n- if Stream: `scalar fallback (Stream cardinality)`\n- else: `offloadable (One|Opt|fold cardinality)`\n\nAnd also note the fused pipeline: if fusion exists, add a note that the pipeline fuses into a kernel loop. Actually, I'll incorporate fusion as an annotation: when the program fuses, the fused stages are \"the offload kernel loop\". But that might over-complicate. Let me keep it simpler and purely cardinality-based, which is exactly what the issue asks for (\"cardinality: One/Opt/fold are admissible, Stream is not\").\n\nLet me now decide on the exact line format and write the code. I'll keep the walker reusing tir's each_node? each_node is private. I'll write my own walker in offload.rs.\n\nLet me write src/offload.rs.\n\n```rust\n//! The `--explain-offload` diagnostic: which sub-expressions are vectorizable\n//! (offloadable) and which fall back to scalar evaluation, and why.\n//!\n//! Vectorizability is silent and cardinality-derived (plans/questions.md Q8): a\n//! sub-expression is admissible for offload exactly when its cardinality is known --\n//! One, Opt, or a fold over a collection -- and not admissible when it is a Stream,\n//! whose extent is unbounded. The design's offload boundary and its layer boundary\n//! are the same boundary (draft.md \"Cardinality is the kernel-admissibility predicate\").\n//!\n//! This is diagnostic-only: it computes nothing the backends do not already decide from\n//! the same types, and it changes neither the type system nor any signature.\n\nuse crate::tir::{Kind, Program, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::Type;\n\n/// The cardinality of an expression, read off its type. Admissibility is a property of\n/// the type, not of the operation (draft.md \"The admissible input set\").\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum Cardinality {\n    /// Exactly one value per input: scalars.\n    One,\n    /// Zero or one: an Opt, admissible as stream compaction.\n    Opt,\n    /// A collection of known extent: a fold over a Vec.\n    Fold,\n    /// Unbounded extent: a Stream. The only inadmissible cardinality.\n    Stream,\n}\n\nimpl Cardinality {\n    pub fn label(self) -> &'static str {\n        match self {\n            Cardinality::One => \"One\",\n            Cardinality::Opt => \"Opt\",\n            Cardinality::Fold => \"fold\",\n            Cardinality::Stream => \"Stream\",\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Whether a sub-expression of this cardinality is admissible for offload. Only a\n    /// Stream is not: its extent is unknown, so it cannot be a kernel and must fall back\n    /// to scalar evaluation.\n    pub fn admissible(self) -> bool {\n        self != Cardinality::Stream\n    }\n}\n\n/// The cardinality an expression carries, from its type. `Stream` is outermost and always\n/// wins when present; otherwise a `Vec` is a fold over known-extent data, an `Opt` is\n/// stream compaction, and anything else is a scalar `One`.\npub fn cardinality(ty: &Type) -> Cardinality {\n    if matches!(ty, Type::Stream(_)) {\n        Cardinality::Stream\n    } else if ty.contains_vec() {\n        Cardinality::Fold\n    } else if ty.as_opt().is_some() {\n        Cardinality::Opt\n    } else {\n        Cardinality::One\n    }\n}\n\n/// A one-line description of one sub-expression's offload disposition.\nfn describe(t: &Tir, depth: usize) -> String {\n    let card = cardinality(&t.ty);\n    let disposition = if card.admissible() {\n        \"offloadable kernel\"\n    } else {\n        \"scalar fallback\"\n    };\n    format!(\n        \"{}{} : {} -> {} -> {}\",\n        \"  \".repeat(depth),\n        kind_name(&t.kind),\n        t.ty,\n        card.label(),\n        disposition\n    )\n}\n\nfn kind_name(k: &Kind) -> String { ... }\n```\n\nHmm wait, I want the output to make the \"why\" clear. Let me make it: `<node>: cardinality <label>; <disposition>`.\n\nNow let me also think: should the walk include the program body and each function? Yes, but the issue's example is a \"small pipeline with both an admissible and a non-admissible sub-expression\". A stream pipeline has a Stream node (source) = non-admissible, and scalar/map-body nodes = admissible. So walking the whole program body gives both.\n\nLet me write the walker to produce a report string:\n\n```rust\npub fn explain(program: &Program) -> String {\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    out.push_str(\"explain offload: vectorizability by cardinality\\n\");\n    for f in &program.funcs {\n        out.push_str(&format!(\"function {}:\\n\", f.name));\n        walk(&f.body, 1, &mut out);\n    }\n    out.push_str(\"program body:\\n\");\n    walk(&program.body, 1, &mut out);\n    out\n}\n```\n\nWhere walk does pre-order with describe().\n\nActually, let me simplify the format to be parseable/stable for the snapshot test. Let me produce lines of the form:\n\n```\noffload: <kind> : <type> -- <cardinality> -- <offloadable kernel|scalar fallback>\n```\n\nLet me also make it clear WHY: for scalar fallback add \"(Stream cardinality: extent unknown, cannot be a kernel)\". For admissible, the cardinality label is the reason. I'll include the cardinality label which IS the reason. Good.\n\nNow, this is a fair amount of new surface. Given AGENTS.md \"build for callers that exist\" and simplicity, maybe I should keep it minimal: a single public function `explain_offload(program: &Program) -> String` plus the CLI flag plus a test. I'll put it in lib.rs directly to avoid a new module? A new module is cleaner and mirrors the repo's module layout. But adding a public module with pub items is more surface. Let me put it in lib.rs as a standalone function plus a small private enum. Actually the repo organizes by module; a new `src/offload.rs` is fine and idiomatic here. But the enum and helpers need to be reachable from the test. The test can call `toylang::offload::explain` if I make it `pub mod offload` with `pub fn explain`. Let me do that.\n\nWait — do I even need a public enum? Only `explain(program) -> String` needs to be public (used by main.rs and the test). I'll keep Cardinality and cardinality() private. Good — minimal public surface: just `offload::explain`.\n\nNow the CLI. Let me update main.rs. The usage line and arg parsing. I'll add handling for `--explain-offload` as the first arg:\n\n```rust\nlet explain = args.first() == Some(&\"--explain-offload\");\nlet args = if explain { &args[1..] } else { args.as_slice() };\n```\n\nThen pass `explain` through to on_file, and in the run/emit/build branches, print the explanation to stderr when explain is set. Actually, let me print to stderr so stdout stays clean (normal output unaffected) — the issue says \"printed alongside normal output, or to stderr\". stderr is cleaner for diagnostics. I'll write the explanation to stderr.\n\nWhere to compute? After compiling. For `run` and `emit`, I have the compiled Program. For `build`, also. Let me compute the program and emit explanation. But currently `run` calls `toylang::run_on` which compiles internally. And `emit` calls `toylang::compile`. Let me restructure to compile once.\n\nSimplest: in on_file, when explain is set and cmd is run/emit/build, compile the src first (which run/emit/build also do), print `toylang::offload::explain(&program)` to stderr, then proceed with normal behavior.\n\nFor `run`: `run(&src, backend)` compiles internally and reads stdin. To avoid compiling twice, I can add an explain path. But `run` needs stdin handling. Let me just compile separately for the explanation (compile is cheap and deterministic) — for run, I'll compile to get the Program for the explanation, print it, then call run normally. Compiling twice is a minor cost for a diagnostic. Actually cleaner: I'll compute the explanation from a compile in the explain branch and print it; the run/emit/build do their own compile. Slight duplication but acceptable and keeps main.rs simple.\n\nLet me restructure on_file to accept `explain: bool`:\n\n```rust\nfn main() {\n    let args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().skip(1).collect();\n    let args: Vec<&str> = args.iter().map(String::as_str).collect();\n    match args.as_slice() {\n        [\"fmt\"] => fmt_project(Mode::Check),\n        [\"fmt\", \"--write\"] => fmt_project(Mode::Write),\n        _ => on_file(&args),\n    }\n}\n```\n\nI'll change to detect --explain-offload:\n\n```rust\nfn main() {\n    let args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().skip(1).collect();\n    let args: Vec<&str> = args.iter().map(String::as_str).collect();\n    if let Some((rest, true)) = strip_flag(&args, \"--explain-offload\") {\n        return on_file(&rest, true);\n    }\n    match args.as_slice() {\n        [\"fmt\"] => fmt_project(Mode::Check),\n        [\"fmt\", \"--write\"] => fmt_project(Mode::Write),\n        _ => on_file(&args, false),\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWhere strip_flag returns (rest, found) if the first arg is the flag. Let me write a small helper. Actually simpler inline:\n\n```rust\nlet (args, explain) = if args.first() == Some(&\"--explain-offload\") {\n    (&args[1..], true)\n} else {\n    (args.as_slice(), false)\n};\nmatch args {\n    [\"fmt\"] => fmt_project(Mode::Check),\n    [\"fmt\", \"--write\"] => fmt_project(Mode::Write),\n    _ => on_file(args, explain),\n}\n```\n\nThen on_file:\n\n```rust\nfn on_file(args: &[&str], explain: bool) -> ExitCode {\n    let (cmd, path, backend) = match args { ... };\n    let src = ...;\n    if explain {\n        // print explanation to stderr\n        match toylang::compile(&src) {\n            Ok(p) => eprint!(\"{}\", toylang::offload::explain(&p)),\n            Err(_) => {} // let the normal path report the error\n        }\n    }\n    let result = match cmd { ... };\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but if compile fails, I shouldn't print a partial explanation; the normal path reports the error. Fine. But also `fmt` with --explain-offload doesn't make sense; the flag only applies to run/emit/build. I'll just compute the explanation for any command that compiles. For fmt it'd print nothing useful. That's acceptable but let me restrict: only when explain is true. Actually let me only attach explanation to run/emit/build. If someone does `--explain-offload fmt`, it just formats. Fine.\n\nWait, there's an issue: the explanation should accompany the actual compiled output. For `run`, printing the explanation to stderr before running is fine. Let me make sure the explanation goes to stderr (not stdout) so it doesn't corrupt captured output / normal output.\n\nNow let me write the test. I'll add a test file `tests/explain_offload.rs` that:\n1. Uses the CLI binary to run with `--explain-offload` on a small pipeline and checks stderr contains the expected annotations.\n2. OR directly calls `toylang::offload::explain` and asserts a snapshot.\n\nThe issue says \"Add a test exercising the flag\". Let me do a CLI-based test that spawns the binary with `--explain-offload run <file> lua` and checks stderr, plus maybe a direct snapshot of explain().\n\nLet me design a test program with both admissible and non-admissible sub-expressions:\n\n```\nfn double(xs: Stream<Int>) -> Stream<Int> =\n    xs | map(. * 2)\n\njsonlines(double(inputs))\n```\n\nHere:\n- `inputs` : Stream<Int> -> Stream cardinality -> scalar fallback (not admissible)\n- the map body `. * 2` (Arith) : Int -> One -> admissible\n- `double(inputs)` call / the pipe: Stream -> not admissible\n- `jsonlines(...)`: Sink -> One -> admissible\n\nGood — has both. But also the sum of known collection is a fold:\n\n```\nsum([1,2,3]) -> Int -> but [1,2,3] is Vec -> fold\n```\n\nLet me write a test that directly checks `explain` output contains both \"offloadable kernel\" and \"scalar fallback\" and the cardinality labels. And a CLI test.\n\nFor the CLI test, I need to read stderr. Let me look at how streaming.rs spawns (I have the pattern). I'll write a simpler test using std::process::Command with the CARGO_BIN_EXE_toylang.\n\nActually, given AGENTS.md's testing philosophy (\"Test behavior that matters... a test file that mirrors source function-for-function is usually testing that code was typed\"), the meaningful test is: the flag produces annotations distinguishing admissible from non-admissible sub-expressions on a program that has both. Let me write a test that exercises the CLI end-to-end (the flag) and asserts the stderr contains the expected cardinality-driven annotations.\n\nLet me now also double check: does `contains_vec` exist on Type? Yes (used in check). And `as_opt` yes. Good.\n\nLet me check Type definition for contains_vec and as_opt signatures. From ty.rs: `as_opt(&self) -> Option<&Type>`, `contains_vec` — let me verify it exists. In check/types.rs `left.ty.contains_vec()`. Let me grep.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a170f10001rJyorNovns70Hd","sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","messageID":"msg_05a166742001IliQhag1GMDwds"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788217856846,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a2cc347329ebc0ee","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/src/ty.rs","limit":70,"offset":100},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/src/ty.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n100: }\n101: \n102: impl Type {\n103:     pub fn from_name(name: &str) -> Option<Type> {\n104:         match name {\n105:             \"Str\" => Some(Type::Str),\n106:             \"Int\" => Some(Type::Int),\n107:             \"Int64\" => Some(Type::Int64),\n108:             \"Bool\" => Some(Type::Bool),\n109:             \"Char\" => Some(Type::Char),\n110:             \"Sink\" => Some(Type::Sink),\n111:             _ => None,\n112:         }\n113:     }\n114: \n115:     /// Whether `ty` could hold a stream anywhere inside it -- through a Vec or a record\n116:     /// field. A stream cannot be printed, so this is what the checker asks before letting a\n117:     /// type become the program's final result.\n118:     pub fn contains_stream(&self) -> bool {\n119:         match self {\n120:             Type::Stream(_) => true,\n121:             Type::Vec(t) => t.contains_stream(),\n122:             Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_stream()),\n123:             // An enum payload cannot hold a stream: resolve_enum refuses the declaration,\n124:             // and instantiation refuses a stream as a type argument.\n125:             _ => false,\n126:         }\n127:     }\n128: \n129:     /// Whether `ty` is a sink or holds one anywhere inside it. A sink is not a value, so no\n130:     /// constructed type may contain one; the type grammar refuses every spelling of `Vec<Sink>`,\n131:     /// a record field, a Stream of one, and an enum payload, and the checker's one general\n132:     /// position rule refuses a bare `Sink` anywhere but a sink position.\n133:     pub fn contains_sink(&self) -> bool {\n134:         match self {\n135:             Type::Sink => true,\n136:             Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => t.contains_sink(),\n137:             Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_sink()),\n138:             Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n139:                 args.iter().any(Type::contains_sink)\n140:                     || variants\n141:                         .iter()\n142:                         .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(Type::contains_sink))\n143:             }\n144:             _ => false,\n145:         }\n146:     }\n147: \n148:     pub fn elem(&self) -> Option<&Type> {\n149:         match self {\n150:             Type::Vec(t) => Some(t),\n151:             _ => None,\n152:         }\n153:     }\n154: \n155:     pub fn field(&self, name: &str) -> Option<&Type> {\n156:         match self {\n157:             Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().find(|(n, _)| n == name).map(|(_, t)| t),\n158:             _ => None,\n159:         }\n160:     }\n161: \n162:     /// The prelude's `Opt<T>`, and what its `T` is. The name test is sound because `Opt` is\n163:     /// the prelude's declaration and a program redeclaring it is a duplicate-type error, so\n164:     /// no other enum can carry the name. This is what every place that used to match the\n165:     /// built-in `Type::Opt` keys on now: the unwrap operator, the producers' wrapping, and\n166:     /// the printers' bare-value-or-null serialization rule.\n167:     pub fn as_opt(&self) -> Option<&Type> {\n168:         match self {\n169:             Type::Enum { name, args, .. } if name == \"Opt\" && args.len() == 1 => Some(&args[0]),\n\n(Showing lines 100-169 of 482. Use offset=170 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"}\n\nimpl Type {\n    pub fn from_name(name: &str) -> Option<Type> {\n        match name {\n            \"Str\" => Some(Type::Str),\n            \"Int\" => Some(Type::Int),\n            \"Int64\" => Some(Type::Int64),\n            \"Bool\" => Some(Type::Bool),\n            \"Char\" => Some(Type::Char),\n            \"Sink\" => Some(Type::Sink),\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Whether `ty` could hold a stream anywhere inside it -- through a Vec or a record\n    /// field. A stream cannot be printed, so this is what the checker asks before letting a\n    /// type become the program's final result.\n    pub fn contains_stream(&self) -> bool {\n        match self {","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/src/ty.rs","text":"}\n\nimpl Type {\n    pub fn from_name(name: &str) -> Option<Type> {\n        match name {\n            \"Str\" => Some(Type::Str),\n            \"Int\" => Some(Type::Int),\n            \"Int64\" => Some(Type::Int64),\n            \"Bool\" => Some(Type::Bool),\n            \"Char\" => Some(Type::Char),\n            \"Sink\" => Some(Type::Sink),\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Whether `ty` could hold a stream anywhere inside it -- through a Vec or a record\n    /// field. A stream cannot be printed, so this is what the checker asks before letting a\n    /// type become the program's final result.\n    pub fn contains_stream(&self) -> bool {\n        match self {\n            Type::Stream(_) => true,\n            Type::Vec(t) => t.contains_stream(),\n            Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_stream()),\n            // An enum payload cannot hold a stream: resolve_enum refuses the declaration,\n            // and instantiation refuses a stream as a type argument.\n            _ => false,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Whether `ty` is a sink or holds one anywhere inside it. A sink is not a value, so no\n    /// constructed type may contain one; the type grammar refuses every spelling of `Vec<Sink>`,\n    /// a record field, a Stream of one, and an enum payload, and the checker's one general\n    /// position rule refuses a bare `Sink` anywhere but a sink position.\n    pub fn contains_sink(&self) -> bool {\n        match self {\n            Type::Sink => true,\n            Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => t.contains_sink(),\n            Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_sink()),\n            Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n                args.iter().any(Type::contains_sink)\n                    || variants\n                        .iter()\n                        .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(Type::contains_sink))\n            }\n            _ => false,\n        }\n    }\n\n    pub fn elem(&self) -> Option<&Type> {\n        match self {\n            Type::Vec(t) => Some(t),\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n\n    pub fn field(&self, name: &str) -> Option<&Type> {\n        match self {\n            Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().find(|(n, _)| n == name).map(|(_, t)| t),\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// The prelude's `Opt<T>`, and what its `T` is. The name test is sound because `Opt` is\n    /// the prelude's declaration and a program redeclaring it is a duplicate-type error, so\n    /// no other enum can carry the name. This is what every place that used to match the\n    /// built-in `Type::Opt` keys on now: the unwrap operator, the producers' wrapping, and\n    /// the printers' bare-value-or-null serialization rule.\n    pub fn as_opt(&self) -> Option<&Type> {\n        match self {\n            Type::Enum { name, args, .. } if name == \"Opt\" && args.len() == 1 => Some(&args[0]),","lineStart":100,"lineEnd":169,"totalLines":482,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/ty.rs","time":{"start":1788217856839,"end":1788217856845}},"id":"prt_05a17120e001UJTu8uiiWGDO7C","sessionID":"ses_fa5eba7fdffec2uHvZPcMB5MvE","messageID":"msg_05a166742001IliQhag1GMDwds"}}
